{"name":"Spanish Viridian","hex":"#007f5c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spanish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To subject to spanishing, a printing process in which an ink is deposited on the bottoms and sides of depressions formed in a plastic material"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spanish"},{"term":"viridian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bluish-green pigment made from chromium sesquioxide.","A bluish-green color."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a bluish green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/viridian"}],"entries":[{"title":"Viridian","blocks":[{"text":"Viridian is a blue-green pigment, a hydrated chromium(III) oxide, of medium saturation and relatively dark in value. It is composed of a majority of green, followed by blue. The first recorded use of viridian as a color name in English was in the 1860s. Viridian takes its name from the Latin viridis, meaning \"green\". The pigment was first prepared in mid-19th-century Paris and remains available from several US manufacturers as prepared artists' colors in all media."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Chromium%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg/330px-Chromium%28III%29-oxide-sample.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viridian"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gouache.jpg/500px-Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gouache.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Viridian%28Pigment_Green_18%29_%28Left%29_And_Phthalocyanine_Green%28Pigment_Green_7%29_%28Right%29_Gouache.jpg/1280px-Viridian%28Pigment_Green_1